                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "First impression: cute casual puzzle game\n\nThis is a sliding puzzle: there's a single hole on the hexagonally tiled board, and you can move pieces into it to move the hole and eventually get Little Red Riding Hood to grandma's house. Bonus if you can do it within the alotted number of moves, but it's not necessary. This gets challenging when you learn that you also have to pick flowers (and a berry bush for bonus), there are immovable obstacles, there are bears (who can be moved) that the girl needs to stay away from, fire that the bears can't come close to, and more -- you get the idea.\n\nThe game is quite well designed, and that applies both to the artwork, the interface, the available settings, and even the tutorial. It is simply enjoyable to play. I have logged 1 hour playing the first 16 of 60 levels, and it's fun shoving the bears hither and yonder to get the red girl to where she needs to go and not waste moves. I expect it would take beginners longer, especially if they want to catch all the stars, but you don't have to: I expect this game is quite accessible to kids (who are more likely to play it on a tablet, though). [i]Update: on level 17 and 18, difficulty gets to medium to 3-star them.[/i]\n\nRecommended.\n\n[i]It runs on my old Windows XP computer, but not quickly.[/i]",
